From 9d0a727a9c99258882293c9af2e314d2bd83ee40 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 05:10:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] forms: Chrome's text control borders, native text field look
 and intrinsic sizes

Text inputs take Chrome's computed defaults, `2px inset rgb(118, 118, 118)`
with `1px 2px` padding, and textareas `1px solid rgb(118, 118, 118)`. Chrome
draws an unstyled text field natively over that border: a 1px line in the
border colour with 2px rounded corners along the border box. The display list
does the same for widgets whose -moz-default-appearance is textfield or
textarea while their appearance is not none and the author has not styled
their border or background (LayoutTheme::IsControlStyled). Image inputs keep
no border, as in Chrome.

The size, cols and rows attributes now reach layout through the internal
-servo-text-control-width/height longhands, which give the control its
intrinsic inline and block size. Widgets already size as fit-content in
block flow, so an auto width resolves to that size plus padding and border
whatever the box-sizing, a percentage width in a shrink-to-fit container
resolves against it, and grid and flex items still stretch, all as in Chrome.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
